The 2017 GMC Yukon has always had a certain reputation.

It’s one of GM’s best-selling full-size SUVs, a go-to choice for families, contractors, and road warriors who want the comfort of a premium ride. At launch, it earned praise for its muscular V8 engine, third-row space, towing capability, and high-end Denali trim.

A lot of people still drive one today. But the 2017 Yukon hasn’t aged without issues.

Between brake problems, airbag system errors, and electrical oddities, this SUV has had its share of problems over the years. Some owners only find out about a recall after something goes wrong. Others are still dealing with lingering symptoms even after “the fix” was supposedly done.

In this post, we’ll break down the most common 2017 GMC Yukon recalls and how to get these issues properly handled. We’ll also explain how the [California lemon law 2025](/blog/whats-changed-a-guide-to-updated-ca-lemon-law) updates affect GMC owners, including the impact of AB 1755 notice requirements and the GM lemon law opt-in.

Let’s start with the recalls themselves.

## Major Recalls Associated With the 2017 GMC Yukon

If you’re still driving a 2017 Yukon, now’s a good time to double-check for any recalls that may have been missed. Even though most of these were announced years ago, there’s a chance the recall work was never completed.

## Airbag System Failure: Frontal Airbags May Not Deploy

In late 2016, GM [issued a recall for certain GMC Yukon and Yukon XL models](https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/rcl/2016/RCSB-16V651-1020.pdf) after discovering a problem with the vehicle’s sensing and diagnostic module, or SDM. The module controls when the airbags and seatbelt pretensioners deploy in a crash.

When the SDM is running the wrong software, these systems may not activate at all. In simple terms, the airbag may fail to deploy in an accident, even when it should.

GM’s repair includes reprogramming or replacing the module at no cost. Because there are usually no warning lights before a collision, this recall is especially important to confirm.

## Brake Assist Loss: Power Brakes Don’t Work

This is one of the [most commonly reported issues tied to the 2017 GMC Yukon](https://www.cnbc.com/2019/09/11/gm-recalls-over-3point4-million-pickups-and-suvs-to-fix-brake-issues.html), and it has to do with how the brake assist system performs over time.

The system relies on a vacuum pump to help deliver consistent stopping power, particularly during low-speed driving. But as the pump ages, it can start to lose strength. When this happens, braking becomes harder, less responsive, and in some cases, downright uncomfortable.

Drivers usually notice:

- The brake pedal feels stiff or slow to respond.

- It takes noticeably more effort to stop, especially in traffic or at low speeds.

- A “Service Brake Assist” message pops up on the dashboard.

- Braking feels jumpy, inconsistent, or unpredictable.

GM rolled out a software update to help the brake system kick in a hydraulic assist when vacuum pressure drops. In some cases, they’ll also replace the vacuum pump if it’s worn out. It’s a no-cost fix, but it won’t undo any damage already caused by hard braking, like worn rotors or brake pads.

## How to Check the Recall Status on Your 2017 GMC Yukon

To confirm whether your 2017 Yukon still has open recalls:

- Enter your VIN on the NHTSA recall lookup site.

- Check GM’s official recall database.

- Schedule service for any recall listed as “Open.”

- Request written confirmation of completed repairs

These records become important later if you pursue a GMC Yukon lemon law claim.

## Transmission Issues in the 2017 Yukon: Widely Reported, But Not Recalled

While the 2017 GMC Yukon hasn’t been recalled for transmission issues, a large number of owners have reported ongoing problems, many of which affect how the vehicle drives and responds under normal conditions.

The most frequently reported symptoms include:

- Sudden jolts or hard shifts, especially when downshifting into second gear

- Rough or delayed gear changes during acceleration or deceleration

- Transmission shudders under light throttle or while cruising.

- Inconsistent shifting behavior, with no clear pattern

These complaints often point to quirks with GM’s 8-speed automatic transmission. GM has released [technical service bulletins (TSBs)](https://www.capitalone.com/cars/learn/managing-your-money-wisely/what-is-a-technical-service-bulletin/1556) instructing dealers on how to reset adaptation settings or update software, but no formal recall has been issued for the 2017 Yukon’s transmission.

Because there’s no safety recall, any repairs related to these transmission problems usually aren’t free unless the vehicle is still under warranty. That said, drivers dealing with repeated transmission issues may still have legal options.

If these transmission problems started under warranty and continue after multiple repair attempts, they may still qualify under the GMC Yukon lemon law, even without a recall.

## Recalls vs. Lemon Law Claims

A recall means GM has acknowledged a defect and is offering a free repair. However, it does not automatically mean [your Yukon qualifies as a lemon](/blog/vehicle-recalls-and-lemon-laws-can-you-get-a-buyback-or-compensation).

Under California law, a vehicle may be considered a lemon when all of the following are true:

- The problem started while the vehicle was under warranty.

- The defect affects the vehicle’s use, value, or safety.

- The manufacturer or dealer was given a reasonable number of chances to fix it.

- The issue still exists, or the vehicle has spent excessive time in the shop.

The “reasonable number of chances” usually looks like this in real life:

- 2 or more repair attempts for a serious safety issue (brakes, airbags, steering, stalling, fire risk)

- 4 or more repair attempts for the same non-safety defect

- 30 or more total days out of service for repairs, even if the issues aren’t identical

If your 2017 GMC Yukon keeps going back for the same brake assist problem, airbag warning, transmission issue, or electrical fault, that pattern matters far more than the recall itself.

## How California Lemon Law Applies in 2025

California’s lemon law 2025 is still one of the strongest in the country, but this year brought some procedural changes that Yukon owners need to be aware of, especially when General Motors is involved.

The biggest update comes from [Assembly Bill 1755](https://www.rvia.org/news-insights/californias-ab-1755-win-consumers-and-rv-industry), which changed how certain lemon law claims must start.

## The New Notice Requirement

Under AB 1755 notice requirements, owners must send GM a formal written notice before filing a lemon law claim. **The notice must include:**

- Your full name

- Your vehicle’s VIN

- A summary of the defect (for example: repeated brake assist failure or transmission problems)

- A clear demand for either a refund or a replacement vehicle

If this notice is not sent, your lemon law rights can be forfeited in cases where the manufacturer has opted into the new system, which GM has.

Since this notice has to be worded correctly and follow specific procedures, many owners choose to have an attorney send it. Most [lemon law attorneys in California](/the-firm) handle this at no cost to you because the manufacturer covers legal fees in valid cases.

> **Important:** [Senate Bill 26](https://calmatters.digitaldemocracy.org/bills/ca_202520260sb26) made this new process optional for carmakers. GM opted in. Some other manufacturers didn’t. Always confirm the manufacturer’s status before filing anything.

## Stricter Deadlines for Filing a Claim

One of the biggest shifts in 2025 is the stricter deadline to bring a lemon law case.

- You now have six years from the vehicle’s original delivery date to file a lemon claim.

- You usually have no more than one year after the warranty ends to take action.

For instance, if your 2017 GMC Yukon was delivered in December 2017, you likely had until December 2023 to file. If you’re just now considering a claim in 2025, it may be too late, unless it was sold to you later under a valid warranty.

## What Counts as a Covered Vehicle in 2025

Recent court decisions have narrowed lemon law coverage for used vehicles, especially those sold “as-is” or with limited warranty language.

This includes:

- New vehicles sold or leased in California

- Manufacturer-certified pre-owned (CPO) vehicles, when sold by a same-brand dealership with a new CPO warranty issued at the time of sale

Here’s what usually no longer qualifies:

- Used vehicles are sold with only the remaining balance of the factory warranty.

- Cars labeled “certified” by independent dealers

- Any third-party warranty that doesn’t come directly from the manufacturer

Essentially, if you bought your 2017 Yukon used and the dealer just passed along the leftover factory warranty, you may not be covered under lemon law anymore. But if you got a fresh CPO warranty from an authorized GMC dealer, you’re likely still protected.

## Why Documentation is More Important Than Ever

California still uses the familiar lemon law presumptions tied to the first 18 months or 18,000 miles from delivery, but manufacturers are scrutinizing paperwork more closely under the updated rules.

To build a strong case, you need to keep all dealership repair records, notes on the dates and mileage of each visit, any “could not duplicate” or “operating as designed” responses, and a log of the time the SUV was out of service.

If a dealership refuses a repair or claims the issue is normal, ask for that in writing. Vague explanations help manufacturers. Detailed records help you.

## Steps to Take If You Suspect Your 2017 GMC Yukon is a Lemon

Repeated breakdowns, warning lights that won’t stay off, and long stretches at the dealership are usually more than bad luck. When a 2017 GMC Yukon keeps showing the same problems after repair attempts, it’s time to shift from hoping the issue disappears to protecting your rights.

## 1) Save Everything

Every service visit matters. Hold on to:

- Repair orders

- Work summaries

- Notes on time spent in the shop

- Comments like “could not duplicate” or “operating as designed.”

This paperwork is the foundation of your lemon law claim. Without it, proving repeated repairs or excessive downtime gets a lot harder.

## 2) Speak Up Early

Don’t sit on a problem hoping it will go away. The sooner you report an issue, the better. Delays weaken your case and make it harder to prove that the problem started while the vehicle was still under warranty.

## 3) Check Eligibility

Not all vehicles qualify for lemon law protection. Ask yourself:

- Was the Yukon purchased or leased new in California within the past six years?

- Was it a manufacturer-certified pre-owned (CPO) purchase from a same-brand dealership, with a new CPO warranty issued at the time of sale?

- Is it still under a valid warranty (factory or CPO) and showing signs of a recurring defect?

If you answered yes to any of those, you may have a valid case.

## 4) Send a Written Notice to GM

Send a formal written notice to General Motors (GM) with your name, VIN, a short description of the defect, and a direct request for a refund or replacement.

## 5) Get in Touch with a Lemon Law Attorney

With the new deadlines in place, automakers are pushing back harder on lemon law claims and counting on owners to miss a step. A seasoned California lemon law attorney can review your repair records, confirm if your Yukon qualifies, and fight for the buyback or replacement you deserve.

## If Your Yukon is Still Giving You Trouble…

The 2017 GMC Yukon remains a capable SUV, but repeated repairs, brake problems, or unresolved transmission issues may point to something more serious.

Check your VIN. Keep detailed records. Understand how the California lemon law 2025 applies to your situation.

